pieshka-polanie-cd-ow2-readme-md.text:
# Polanie CD 4.27 Source Code with the level editor for Open Watcom 2.0

This repository contains compilable source code for the original [Polanie CD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polanie_(video_game)) release, version 4.27 and its level editor.

The 1997 release was written and compiled in Watcom 10.6 which is a bit older compiler (the level editor in Watcom 10.0). Current MS-DOS-alike systems like FreeDOS uses Open Watcom for C/C++ development.

This is my attempt to port Polanie CD to OW2, which - unfortunately - is incompatible out-of-the-box.

## Compiling and running

To compile this you need to download the Open Watcom 2.0 suite, open `pol2.wpj` (or `edit2.wpj` for the editor) in the Open Watcom IDE and hit `Make All`. The resulting `pol2.exe` (`edit2.exe`) can be placed in game directory and executed.
Tested on DOSBOX.

IMPORTANT! To use the level editor you need to copy `FONT.DAT` and `LEVELS` directory from the original Polanie disc. The editor cannot run from the CD.

## Contributing

This repository is not for fixing the original game. If you encounter issuses that are not preset in the original executable, you can file a issue. Please attach your save file.
I won't be fixing original game's bugs.

## Changes
Only simple maintenance changes were required with the exeception of the `delay` function.

Watcom 10.6 allowed writing code like this:
```c++
for(int i = 0; ... )
{}

for(i = 0; ... )
{}
```

which is not allowed in Open Watcom 2 (and many modern compilers), so it needed to be rewritten:
```c++
int i = 0;

for(i = 0; ... )
{}

for(i = 0; ... )
{}
```

One instance of weird behavior was observed in cd.cpp where code like this:
```c++
int i = 0;

for(int i = 0; i < ...; i++)
{}

doing sth with i
```
would increment also the outer-scope. OW2 doesn't work like this so it needed to be rewritten like this:
```c++
int i = 0;

for(i = 0; i < ...; i++)
{}

doing sth with i
```

And the biggest obstacle. The algorithm for `delay()` function was changed in OpenWatcom and its quantized to 55ms. Watcom 10.6's implementation for `delay()` allowed 1ms precision.
Fortunately Polanie uses 100,300,500 ms delays... besides four functions, where 1ms delay is used. So I created simple stub delayms.h to use in this few rare cases (image13h.cpp).
Other .cpp files are using standard OW's implementation of `delay()`.

## License

This source code was granted to the maintainer for the `polanie.prv.pl` site without any license. So you know... It is not technically open-source. More like, a very good decompilation.
(Very good because it's copy-paste from the author's hard drive). More info [here](https://github.com/Pieshka/polanie-cd-wc106)

